Thanks @loicmathieu  for your quick response.

I understand that `stream()` method needs a transaction. The problem is there is one. I changed my code to this:

```java
public Set<MyDomainObject> getThingsCalled(String name)
   try {
      System.out.println("THREAD: " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
      System.out.println("TRANSACTION STATUS: " + transactionManager.getStatus());
      System.out.println("TRANSACTION STATUS: " + transactionManager.getTransaction().getStatus());
   } catch (SystemException e) {
      e.printStackTrace();
   }

   return MyEntity.<MyEntity>find("name", name).stream()
      .map(entity -> entity.toDomainObject())
      .collect(Collectors.toSet());
}
```
And I see this in my logs:
```
THREAD: pool-15-thread-5
TRANSACTION STATUS: 0
TRANSACTION STATUS: 0
2020-03-02 11:18:24,382 WARN  [io.agr.pool] (pool-15-thread-5) Datasource '<default>': Closing open connection prior to commit
2020-03-02 11:18:24,382 WARN  [io.agr.pool] (pool-15-thread-5) Datasource '<default>': JDBC resources leaked: 1 ResultSet(s) and 1 Statement(s)
```

As far as I can tell 
 - there is a transaction in place and it is ACTIVE
 - both my code and `stream()` are executed by the same thread 

Am I missing something obvious here?

Easy way to reproduce: 

1) get the [hibernate-orm-panache-quickstart](https://github.com/quarkusio/quarkus-quickstarts/tree/master/hibernate-orm-panache-quickstart)
2) add the following to `FruitResource`

```java
@Inject protected TransactionManager tm;
private ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(5);

@GET
@Path("/called/{name}")
public void find(@PathParam String name, @Suspended AsyncResponse asyncResponse) {
   executor.execute(() -> {
      try {
         tm.begin();
         Set<String> fruitNames = Fruit.<Fruit>find("name", name).stream()
            .map(f -> f.name)
            .collect(Collectors.toSet());
         tm.commit();
         asyncResponse.resume(Response.ok().entity(fruitNames).build());
      } catch (Throwable e) {
         try {
            tm.rollback();
         } catch (Exception rbe) {
           rbe.printStackTrace();
        }
     }
   });	
}
```

3) run dev mode and go to `localhost:8080/fruits/called/a`

I personally see no reason why such code should result in warning. But as I said I may be missing something obvious here.

If you close the stream the warning message will go away:

```java
@Inject protected TransactionManager tm;
private ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(5);

@GET
@Path("/called/{name}")
public void find(@PathParam String name, @Suspended AsyncResponse asyncResponse) {
   executor.execute(() -> {
      try {
         tm.begin();
         Stream<Fruit> stream = Fruit.<Fruit>find("name", name).stream();
         Set<String> fruitNames = stream
            .map(f -> f.name)
            .collect(Collectors.toSet());
         stream.close();
         tm.commit();
         asyncResponse.resume(Response.ok().entity(fruitNames).build());
      } catch (Throwable e) {
         try {
            tm.rollback();
         } catch (Exception rbe) {
           rbe.printStackTrace();
        }
     }
   });	
}```
